# Real signing material and credentials must NEVER be committed. A keystore
# or a password pushed to git history is effectively unrecoverable the
# instant it's pushed — treat any leak here as a full key compromise, not a
# mistake you can quietly clean up (see RELEASE.md for exactly why).
#
# Order matters in this file: the broad ignore patterns come first, and the
# `!keystore.properties.example` negation comes LAST so it wins. If this file
# is ever reordered so the negation sits before a pattern that matches it,
# git silently drops the example from tracking again — keep the negation at
# the bottom.

# The real, filled-in credentials file. Never commit this.
keystore.properties

# Keystore / key material, in any of the common formats.
*.jks
*.keystore
*.p12

# Keep the template tracked so contributors can see the expected keys.
!keystore.properties.example
